## 2. Spec Axis — Canonical Wiring, Live OODA, No Toy Crypto

### 2.1 Canonical import adoption on entry

| Import | L2 daemons importing it on entry | Of 15 |
| :--- | :---: | :--- |
| `std/core/ooda.oo` (canonical 4-phase OODA turn) | 6 | 9 daemons had **no** canonical OODA turn at all |
| `std/sec/pqc.oo` (canonical ML-DSA-65-style sponge, sign, HMAC) | **0** | every daemon used the toy modulus instead |
| `std/sec/capreg.oo` (canonical 64-bit capability register) | 6 | 9 daemons carried a local copy of the register arithmetic |
| `std/sec/warrant.oo` (canonical hardware warrants) | 6 | 9 daemons carried a local copy of the warrant arithmetic |

### 2.2 The toy modulus

`grep -rl 1000000007` over the L2 batch found the toy prime modulus in **every** daemon, in both security primitives and plain
checksums:

| Site | Count of files | What it was |
| :--- | ---: | :--- |
| `hw/attest.oo` | 15 | Attestation digest, keyed HMAC, and ML-DSA-style signature |
| `core/warrant.oo` | 15 | The service warrant seal |
| `core/wire.oo` and the per-daemon frame codecs | 7 | The word-7 IPC frame integrity tag |
| `core/types.oo`, `core/policy.oo` and the per-daemon equivalents | 22 | Table checksum and name fingerprint |
| `core/aid_*`, `core/realm_*`, `realm/*`, `svcmgr/*`, `dns/*`, `policy/*` | 15 | Ring checksum, state checksum, frame codec |

The canonical L0 pages already owned every one of these primitives — `std/sec/pqc.oo` (`oo_pqc_sha3_round`, `oo_pqc_sign`,
`oo_pqc_hmac`, 62-bit, explicitly "zero toy prime moduli"), `std/ipc/wire.oo` (`oo_wire_mac`, the canonical 62-bit SplitMix64
IPC MAC), `std/sec/capreg.oo`, and `std/sec/warrant.oo`. The daemons were copying them instead of importing them.

### 2.3 The vacuous differential-parity gate

Six daemons (`ooaid`, `oohealthd`, `ooimage`, `ooinit`, `oonetd`, `oosession`) imported canonical `std/sec/capreg.oo` and then
asserted in their live capability gate:

```
    if packed != oo_cap_reg64_pack(7, 3, 4, mask) { return false; }
    if child != oo_cap_reg64_attenuate(packed, 1, 9, 1) { return false; }
```

Each line compares a value with the function that produced it, so the comparison can never be false and the gate is dead weight
that reads as a differential-parity check. The same pattern was introduced in `ooaid` by commit `0834d20` and was still present
on entry.

### 2.4 The lost parity oracle (ecosystem-wide)

`std-hw` commit `90b8cd1` ("fix(capreg): delegate the capability register helpers to std/sec/capreg.oo", 2026-11-05 07:19 -1000)
changed `std-hw/hw/runtime/rt_caps_reg64.oo` from an independent implementation into thin aliases:

```
-pub fn rt_cap_reg64_pack(epoch: Int, rights: Int, badge: Int, caps_mask: Int) -> Int {
-    return ((epoch & 65535) << 48) | ((rights & 255) << 40) | ((badge & 255) << 32) | (caps_mask & 4294967295);
+pub fn rt_cap_reg64_pack(epoch: Int, rights: Int, badge: Int, caps_mask: Int) -> Int {
+    return oo_cap_reg64_pack(epoch, rights, badge, caps_mask);
```

`std/sec/capreg.oo` already carried the same aliases. After `90b8cd1` there is **no independent implementation of the register
arithmetic left at the `.oo` level**, so every assertion of the form `local != rt_cap_reg64_pack(...)` compares a value against
the function that produced it. That includes the parity assertion that `mcode-review 1` restored in `oosysbus/core/capreg.oo` as
its defect D1.

### 2.5 Live execution

`ooaudiod` and the other nine Generation-B daemons had no canonical OODA turn, and their capability gate was reached from the
live policy path (`core/policy.oo`, `if <PFX>_cap_reg_ok() == false { return false; }`) but only checked the register. After
remediation every daemon runs the canonical `oo_ooda_init → observe → orient → decide → act` turn over its own 32-capability
register inside that same live gate.
